---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'
import StripChart from '@/components/StripChart.astro'
import Waterfall from '@/components/Waterfall.astro'
import RunDrum from '@/components/RunDrum.astro'
import LeaseBoard from '@/components/LeaseBoard.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import SortingBench from '@/components/SortingBench.astro'
import Conformance from '@/components/Conformance.astro'
import AuditLedger from '@/components/AuditLedger.astro'
import { SITE } from '@/lib/site'
import { CHANNELS, spell } from '@/lib/conformance'
---

<Base title="mcpd" description={SITE.description} kind="software" og="default" flushFooter>
  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 01
      The fold. A full-width recessed instrument with the claim on the panel
      above it and the one action beneath. Not a hero stack; not a text column
      beside a product card.

      `min-h-svh` so the first screen is always this composition and never a
      band of the next section peeking in underneath it. `svh` rather than `vh`
      because mobile browsers shrink the visual viewport when the toolbar
      retracts, and `vh` would leave a gap at exactly the moment someone starts
      to scroll. */}
  <section class="flex min-h-[calc(100svh-3.9rem)] flex-col justify-center gap-8 py-10">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[86rem] px-5 sm:px-8">
      <div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-6 max-lg:block">
        <h1 class="display max-w-[17ch] text-[clamp(2.4rem,5.6vw,4.5rem)]">
          Every step your agent takes, held to the end and on the record.
        </h1>
        {/* One paragraph. It shipped as two, differing by a breakpoint and by
            four words, and the clause that answers the reader's actual
            objection had already gone missing from the mobile copy. The measure
            changes at the breakpoint; the sentence does not. */}
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[46ch] pb-2 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2 lg:mt-0 lg:max-w-[30ch]">
          mcpd is a daemon, not a framework. It holds a run between steps, the
          retries, the timers, the week-long wait for an approval, while your
          own code does the work inside each step, in any language. Every step
          lands in a trace with what it cost.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[86rem] px-5 sm:px-8">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between pb-2">
        <span class="legend">Model latency</span>
        <span class="data hidden text-[0.75rem] text-ink-3 sm:inline">specimen trace</span>
      </div>
      {/* Shorter on a phone. At a fixed 300 the instrument took half the
          viewport and pushed the command, the only action on this page, below
          the fold on a 667px screen.

          The pen arm moves in with it. It stands a fixed distance inside the
          field, and at the desktop distance it sat off the right-hand edge of
          every phone: the paper fed past nothing and the signature instrument
          read as a static line. At 200 it stands about two thirds of the way
          across a 320px screen and still inside a 640px one. */}
      <StripChart height={170} pen={200} class="sm:hidden" />
      <StripChart height={300} class="max-sm:hidden" />
    </div>

    {/* One action. The command is the call to action; there is no button pair.

        The well and the sentence sit side by side only from `xl`. At `lg` the
        sentence had no measure and took what it wanted, and the well, the only
        action on the page, was squeezed to one word per line on a 1024 screen.
        Below `xl` the sentence sits under the well at a reading measure. */}
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[86rem] px-5 sm:px-8">
      <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-4 xl:flex-row xl:items-center">
        {/* The command that works today. The Docker image is the intended
            install and lands with the first release; until then a page that
            led with it sent every reader to a pull that fails. */}
        <Command
          class="xl:max-w-[38rem] xl:flex-1"
          label="Build and run it"
          command="git clone https://github.com/mcpd/mcpd && cd mcpd && make build && ./target/release/mcpd demo"
        />
        <p class="max-w-[46ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2 xl:pl-2">
          Then open <span class="data text-ink">localhost:4317</span>. No account, no
          database to stand up, nothing to sign. No release yet: the image and the
          packages land with the first one.
          <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="link ml-1">Read the five-minute quickstart</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 02
      Prove it. The product doing its actual job on a real failing run. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,22rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <h2 class="display text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
          A run you can read.
        </h2>
        <p class="prose mt-5 text-[0.9375rem]">
          An agent that fails does not fail in the log line that mentions the
          error. It fails three steps earlier, when the model chose the wrong
          tool and then chose it again.
        </p>
        <p class="prose mt-4 text-[0.9375rem]">
          mcpd reconstructs the whole run as a tree: every model call, every tool
          call, every retrieval, with the exact prompt and completion attached to
          each, the latency of each, and what each one cost.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6">
          <a href="/app" class="link">Open the interface</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Waterfall />
    </div>
    <RunDrum class="mt-10" />
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 02b
      Hold it. The daemon between the steps: the argument the run cursor makes,
      with the instrument taking the full width because the drawing is the
      argument and the copy is its caption. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-x-10 gap-y-4">
      <h2 class="display max-w-[22ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
        A run that survives the process that started it.
      </h2>
      <p class="prose max-w-[46ch] text-[0.9375rem]">
        The daemon owns the run between steps. Your process owns everything
        inside one, and hands nothing over: not the model, not the prompt, not
        a credential.
      </p>
    </div>

    <LeaseBoard class="mt-10" />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">The graph is data, the bodies are yours</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          A workflow is written once, in code. The graph goes to the daemon as
          a definition it validates and versions. The step bodies stay in your
          process, in any of six languages, for a worker to serve.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Retries, timers and waits are rows</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          A transient failure is retried on the step's own budget. A sleep or a
          wait for an approval holds the run with no process alive, through a
          deploy, a crash, or a week. An event answers the wait by name and
          key, and a repeated event is a duplicate, not a second run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Every run is a trace</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          Each step is a span with its input, its output and the usage it
          reported, priced with the same book that prices every other span.
          The model call inside a step sits under it, so the search, the cost
          and the scorers work on a run unchanged.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8">
      <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Read about workflows</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 03
      Instrument it. Real code, in every language, read from the examples. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,32rem)] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <h2 class="display max-w-[22ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
          Instrument in the language you already write.
        </h2>
        <p class="prose mt-5 text-[0.9375rem]">
          Six SDKs, one behaviour. Recording a span never blocks, never throws,
          and never grows without bound: if mcpd is unreachable your agent does
          not notice, which is the only acceptable way for observability to fail.
          Each one also carries a worker, so the steps of a durable run are
          served from the same process that records them.
        </p>
        <p class="prose mt-4 text-[0.9375rem]">
          None of them is required. Point <span class="data text-ink"
            >OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</span
          > at mcpd and an OpenTelemetry SDK you already run reaches it, with no code
          of ours in your application. The six are a convenience over an HTTP surface,
          not a condition of being recorded.
        </p>
        {/* The honest difference from observability that ships inside a
            framework, stated here rather than in a section of its own: this is
            the paragraph where a reader is deciding whether their existing
            agent qualifies. */}
        <p class="prose mt-4 text-[0.9375rem]">
          Observability that arrives attached to a framework can only record
          agents built with that framework. mcpd takes whatever reaches its OTLP
          endpoint: an agent framework, a loop you wrote yourself, or the
          services either one calls.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
          <a href="/docs/sdks/typescript" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">TypeScript</a>
          <a href="/docs/sdks/python" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">Python</a>
          <a href="/docs/sdks/rust" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">Rust</a>
          <a href="/docs/sdks/go" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">Go</a>
          <a href="/docs/sdks/kotlin" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">Kotlin</a>
          <a href="/docs/sdks/elixir" class="link text-[0.9375rem]">Elixir</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <CodeSwitcher region="trace" title="instrument a run" />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 04
      What it records. Six signals, one correlation, shown as a diagram of
      the actual mechanism, not as three feature cards. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="display max-w-[26ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
      Six signals, correlated by the run that produced them.
    </h2>
    <p class="prose mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-[0.9375rem]">
      Most tools give you traces here and logs somewhere else, and leave you to
      match timestamps by eye. mcpd stores all six signals against the same
      trace and span ids, so a spike on a chart opens the run that caused it and
      a run shows the log lines it emitted.
    </p>
    <SortingBench class="mt-10" />
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 05
      Proof. The one claim a competitor cannot answer with better copy, drawn as
      the instrument that checks it.

      The reading order here is instrument first, argument after, and that is
      the point of the section rather than a variation for its own sake. Three
      sections in a row had put a text column on the left and an instrument on
      the right; a third instance of one skeleton stops being a rhythm and
      starts being a template. It was also the wrong home for this particular
      instrument. Seven channels against nine checks is a wide reading, and in a
      right-hand column it had to scroll sideways inside itself on any screen
      under about 1200px, so the last three checks were behind a scrollbar
      exactly where the argument lands. At full width the whole grid is on
      screen at once, which is the only form in which it argues anything: you
      are meant to see that every cell is lit, not read a sentence saying so. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    {/* The number is read from the harness, not typed here. A language added
        to the suite used to leave this headline saying seven beside a panel
        drawing eight rows. */}
    <h2 class="display max-w-[20ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
      {spell(CHANNELS.length)} languages that agree.
    </h2>

    <Conformance class="mt-9" />

    <div class="mt-9 grid gap-x-14 gap-y-4 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <p class="max-w-[52ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
        Every SDK is tested on its own, which proves each does what its authors
        meant and nothing about whether they match. So each one writes the same
        run against one daemon, and the traces are read back and compared to
        each other.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-[52ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
        A behaviour that holds in one language and not another shows up there
        and nowhere else. It runs in CI on every change.
        <a href="/docs/from-mastra" class="link ml-1">How this compares</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 05a
      Running it for other people. The composition is deliberately not the
      text-beside-instrument of the two sections around it: the ledger takes the
      full width, because the ledger IS the argument.

      The notes under it answer the questions a review asks after the ledger,
      and they are notes rather than sections on purpose. Attribution to a
      person, erasure by subject and `mcpd doctor` each landed as a feature and
      each could have taken a section of its own; a page that grows a section
      per feature is a feature list, and this one is not. They belong here
      because they all answer the same question the ledger does. There are six,
      which is two full rows of the three-across grid: the count has to stay a
      multiple of three, because a seventh note leaves one alone on a third row
      and that reads as an accident rather than a decision. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-x-10 gap-y-4">
      <h2 class="display max-w-[20ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem)]">
        Who read what, including who was turned away.
      </h2>
      <p class="prose max-w-[42ch] text-[0.9375rem]">
        The question that decides a security review is not whether the data is
        safe. It is whether you can show, afterwards, who reached it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AuditLedger class="mt-10" />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Six key scopes</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          Emitting telemetry, reading prompts, changing stored data, destroying
          it, starting runs and claiming steps are different privileges. An
          unset write list accepts the ingest keys, and an unset admin list
          accepts the read keys. Unset execute and worker lists accept the write
          keys. Upgrading does not lock you out.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">A project is a boundary</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          Keys, retention and sampling are all per project. A key listed under
          one reaches that one, and the daemon resolves who owns a dataset
          before it returns a single case from it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Nothing calls home</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          Four outbound request sites exist in the whole daemon, and you
          configure every one of them: the forwarder, two webhooks and the
          judge. There is no update check, no telemetry and no licence server.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Which key, and which person</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          The ledger names the key. Give mcpd the header your ingress already
          sets, and the addresses allowed to set it, and it names the person as
          well. Sent from anywhere else, a header is a claim anyone could make,
          so the row stays empty rather than carry a guess.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">A deletion request is one call</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          One DELETE removes every run recorded against a person, with the
          spans, logs, metrics, scores and feedback hanging off them, and
          answers with a count per signal. Rows in a project that key cannot
          open are neither touched nor counted.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink">Check it before you trust it</p>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[38ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <span class="data text-ink">mcpd doctor</span> grades a deployment:
          whether the store takes a write and gives it back, whether a scope was
          left open, whether a configured project has ever received a span.
          Anything it wants you to act on names the flag that fixes it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 05b
      The limits, on the page rather than buried in a file. Three independent
      reviewers named this as the single most trust-building thing available to
      a project with no customers, and it costs nothing but nerve. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ channel 06
      The close, on the panel's darker floor. */}
  <div class="seam"></div>
  <section class="bg-floor text-floor-ink">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[86rem] px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
      <div class="grid items-center gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,34rem)]">
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <h2 class="display max-w-[16ch] text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.75rem)] text-floor-ink">
            Five minutes to your first trace.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-[46ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-floor-ink/80">
            Run the daemon, point your agent at it, open the interface. If it
            takes longer than that, it is a bug and we would like the report.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Command
          variant="floor"
          label="Start"
          command="docker run -p 4317:4317 ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd"
          secondary={[
            { label: 'Kubernetes', command: 'export INGEST_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) READ_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) && helm install mcpd oci://ghcr.io/mcpd/charts/mcpd -n mcpd --create-namespace --set keys.ingest=$INGEST_KEY --set keys.read=$READ_KEY' },
            { label: 'From source', command: 'git clone https://github.com/mcpd/mcpd && cd mcpd && make dist' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
